The following C# code snippet represents the relevant output assert statements corresponding to the comments that start with “@@@” in the provided code:

```cs

Debug.Assert((Old_index < Old_arrListCount && Old_index >= 0), "Incorrect index");

Debug.Assert((New_arrListCount == Old_arrListCount), "Incorrect array count");

Debug.Assert((New_containsNewRet == true), "New element not correctly inserted");

```

Please note that to use the Debug.Assert function in C#, you have to include System.Diagnostics at the top of your source file:

```cs
using System.Diagnostics; 
``` 

This function will check a condition and break the execution of your program if the condition is false. The string after the comma is the message to display when the assertion fails.--------------------Here are the assert statements corresponding to the comments mentioned in the code:

```cs
Debug.Assert(Old_index < Old_arrListCount || Old_index == Old_arrListCount);
```

This says "Old index is less than or same as old array count" as mentioned in the comment.

Here is the complete `ArrayListContractTest` class with the assert statements that have been added:

```cs
namespace ArrayList.Test
{
    public partial class ArrayListContractTest
    {
        public void PUT_GetContract([PexAssumeUnderTest]ArrayList arrList, int index)
        {
            AssumePrecondition.IsTrue(index < arrList.Count && index >= 0);

            int Old_arrListCount = arrList.Count;
            int Old_index = index;

            int New_Ret = (int)(arrList[index]);

            int New_arrListCount = arrList.Count;
            bool New_containsNewRet = arrList.Contains(New_Ret);

            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$old_arrList_Count", Old_arrListCount);
            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$new_arrList_Count", New_arrListCount);
            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$old_arrList_index", Old_index);
            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$New_ret", New_Ret);

            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$new_containsNewRet", New_containsNewRet);

            Debug.Assert(Old_index < Old_arrListCount || Old_index == Old_arrListCount); // this is a comment
        }
    }
}
```
Note:
1. `PexObserve.ValueForViewing` is not an Assert but is used to check the values for debugging purposes. It does not stop the test if a condition is not met.
2. `AssumePrecondition.IsTrue` is a PEX customization for code contracts. It does not stop the test if a condition is not met; it only informs PEX about the condition being met or not.
3. `Debug.Assert` is used for asserting debug conditions. It stops the execution of the program if the assertion if found to be false.--------------------The corresponding output assert statements would be:

```csharp
            //Old_arrListCount is lower or equal to arrList.Count
            Debug.Assert(Old_arrListCount <= arrList.Count);

            //Old_index is positive
            Debug.Assert(Old_index >= 0);

            //New_arrListCount is lower or equal to arrList.Count
            Debug.Assert(New_arrListCount <= arrList.Count);

            //New_containsNewRet is only true when New_Ret is part of arrList
            Debug.Assert(New_containsNewRet == arrList.Contains(New_Ret));
```
The code in the comment is a condition that must hold. These correspond to the assert statements that make sure these conditions hold. The `arrList.Count` and `index` are assumed not to be negative in the original code. 

These assertions can be used in conjunction with your test to check whether the conditions are satisfied during execution.--------------------Without seeing the contents of PexObserve, it's not possible to generate C# code with Debug.Assert statements as that is highly context-dependent. It would make much more sense to add debug checks with a debugger or a logging framework.

However, assuming we can infer that "@@@ old index is less than or same as old array count" is meant to be a Debug.Assert statement, the corresponding C# code could be:

```cs
Debug.Assert(Old_index <= Old_arrListCount, "Old index should be less than or equal to old array list count.");
```
This makes an assertion that if the old index is not less than or equal to the count of the list, it will raise an exception. Note this assumes you're using a version of .NET with System.Diagnostics.Debug in its namespace.--------------------Here are the assertion statements in C# code extracted from an example:

```csharp
Debug.Assert(Old_index < Old_arrListCount, "old index is less than old array count");
Debug.Assert(Old_index <= Old_arrListCount, "old index is less than or same as old array count");
Debug.Assert(New_containsNewRet, "new array list contains New_ret");
```

Please note that the C# `Debug.Assert()` method checks if a condition is true. If it is true, nothing happens. If it is false, a System.Diagnostics.AssertionFailedException is thrown with the specified message.

The message is displayed when your application is running in a Debug build. When your application is running in a Release build, all Debug.Assert statements are removed and no exceptions are thrown.--------------------The C# code with Debug.Assert statements corresponding to the comments that start with "@@@" in the provided code snippet is:

```cs
using System.Diagnostics;

namespace ArrayList.Test
{
    public partial class ArrayListContractTest
    {
        public void PUT_GetContract([PexAssumeUnderTest]ArrayList arrList, int index)
        {
            Debug.Assert(index < arrList.Count && index >= 0); // @@@ old index is less than or same as old array count

            AssumePrecondition.IsTrue(index < arrList.Count && index >= 0);

            int Old_arrListCount = arrList.Count;
            int Old_index = index;

            int New_Ret = (int)(arrList[index]);

            int New_arrListCount = arrList.Count;
            bool New_containsNewRet = arrList.Contains(New_Ret);

            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$old_arrList_Count", Old_arrListCount);
            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$new_arrList_Count", New_arrListCount);
            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$old_arrList_index", Old_index);
            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$New_ret", New_Ret);

            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$new_containsNewRet", New_containsNewRet);
        }
    }
}
```

Note: This code seems to be written specifically for PEX (Path Coverage) library used for testing in unit testing or fuzz testing. You would need to adapt the code to work according to your specific testing environment. Moreover, these Debug.Assert statements help to debug the csharp code. They are asserting the conditions before their execution (like checking preconditions before the main logic), therefore they are useful for verifying the correctness of your code.--------------------Using the Regex (`System.Text.RegularExpressions`) namespace, we will be able to capture those comments that start with "@@@". The following code will extract these comments and create `Debug.Assert` statements for each of them. The extracted comments will then be outputted in the console.

```cs

using System;
using System.Text.RegularExpressions;
using System.Diagnostics;

namespace ArrayList.Test
{
    class Program
    {
        static void Main(string[] args)
        {
            string code = 
            @"
                // @@@ old index is less than or same as old array count
            ";

            // Match the pattern @@@ and captures everything until the next line break.
            string pattern = @"// @@@\s*(?<comment>.+?)\n";
            
            MatchCollection matches = Regex.Matches(code, pattern, RegexOptions.Singleline);

            foreach(Match match in matches)
            {
                // Extract the comment
                Group commentGroup = match.Groups["comment"];
                string comment = commentGroup.Value.Trim();

                // Create a debug.assert statement
                string debugAssert = $"Debug.Assert(condition, comment);";
                debugAssert = debugAssert.Replace("condition", "false");
                debugAssert = debugAssert.Replace("comment", $"\"{comment}\"");

                Console.WriteLine(debugAssert);
            }
        }
    }
}

```

Remember to replace `code` with your C# code, the code above is just an example.

Please note that the code above will output Debug.Assert(false, "old index is less than or same as old array count"); and replace `condition` with your assertion condition and `comment` with your comment.

This is a placeholder, you're going to replace `condition` and `comment` with the relevant parts for your tests.

Also, Regex can't parse every type of C# code. It can only parse specific types of patterns. If your C# code is more complex and uses constructs that aren't easily parsed by regular expressions, you may need to write a more complex program to parse your code.--------------------The output assert statements are:

```cs
